{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.OrderTruncationGeometricTail","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.OrderTruncationGeometricTail","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.tail_lt_of_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.tail_lt_of_ratio","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrderTruncationGeometricTail.lean#L107-L133","doc":"**Truncation at an explicit order.** With a contraction ratio `r < 1`, every\norder beyond some finite point contributes less than any prescribed `ε > 0`. The\norder is explicit: any `k` with `r ^ (k - N) * a N < ε`.\n\nThe super-exponential case of the `w_std` bound is stronger than this — there the\neffective ratio itself tends to `0` — so this is the conservative form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.le_geometric_of_ratio'","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.le_geometric_of_ratio'","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrderTruncationGeometricTail.lean#L98-L105","doc":"The same bound indexed by an order `k ≥ N` rather than an offset. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.le_geometric_of_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.le_geometric_of_ratio","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/OrderTruncationGeometricTail.lean#L77-L96","doc":"**Geometric domination from a ratio bound.** If each order past `N` is at most\n`r` times the previous one, then order `N + j` is at most `r^j` times order `N`.\n\nThis is the step that licenses dropping high orders: a per-order ratio bound is\nusually what one can establish, while a *tail* bound is what truncation needs. ","signatureChunk":0}]}